A||||24||deceler8||How to...clear hard drive & redo Win95||||1||
Z||000000||deceler8||06-20-2001||10:28 AM||||Seems like this computer (Gateway Pentium with Win95) a friend picked up has a lot of corporate crap like passwords and stuff that make it unusable from a home standpoint.<P>I had a problem getting it to work with a certain monitor, but find that there is no provision for changing any of the settings on this version of Windows 95.<P>I would just like to wipe all the old info off the hard drive on this computer and reinstall a new copy of Windows 95 (which I have).<P>What's the best way to go about this ?<P>And of course, thanks in advance for any help that can be provided.||||reg||1
Z||000001||84Bill||06-20-2001||10:38 AM||||Well you came to the right place, I'm on helpdesk call today     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <BR>If you have a 98 CD and a CD ROM equiped PC just slip the CD in and boot up the PC this will start the install. If it does boot to the CD then do the steps below to format the C Drive.<P>If it does not "Boot to CD" then start windows and then click start, Shutdown & Exit to DOS.<P>Check to see if you have a CD Drive (usually d    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>If you do your in fat city then just go to the C Drive and type "format c: /q /s" without the quotes and press enter.<BR>When the format is done (DO NOT RESTART!) go to the CD Drive and type "setup" and press enter. the setup should start<P>Just follow thw prompts and just keep clicking next, don't worry about all the settings and stuff.<P>You have done well Grasshopper<P>------------------<BR>This is a copy of RiceCooker's witty signature,<BR>It makes me a lemming<P><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-20-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000002||LarryB||06-20-2001||10:41 AM||||There are a couple of different ways about it but they're all basically variants of the same idea:  boot off an alternate media, reformat the hard disk, and do a reinstall.  Don't bother with trying to overlay an installation over the existing install. Reformatting is a clean and easy way to blow away all the old data.<P>You should go to the Gateway site and download any drivers you'll need onto floppies before starting. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||DecadenceR||06-20-2001||11:39 AM||||If I can make a suggestion, go guy a copy of Win ME if you can afford to. It runs better, less problems.<BR>But definitely formatting and re-installing is the best way to go. Make sure you do have all the other drivers that you need before you delete anything.||||reg||1
Z||000004||overlord||06-20-2001||12:49 PM||||heres my 2 cents on WINME.<BR>I decided to upgrade to it, installed it, everything went smooth...but then noticed that it crashed waaay too much...and was generally unstable.  It could of been just my system, but after going back to win98 se...everything purrs.<P>Ive heard  other horror stories of win me also.......||||reg||1
