Z||000010||Formula88||06-20-2001||03:57 PM||||Well, my .02 as a Computer Analyst is to avoid WinME like the plague.  At least until they get some better service packs for it.  Too buggy.  Win98 Second Edition is my recommendation for home use.  Booting from the CD is a good idea. Most PC's will do it.  <B>But before you start---</B> make sure you make a Windows Startup Disk!!!!  Once you blow away your HDD, if you can't boot to the CD, you're screwed if you don't have a boot diskette.  Other than that, format the drive, and install the OS on a clean drive straight from the CD.  If you have any other questions, feel free to ask.<P>One other tip, on Windows CD's there is a directory called "CABS" .  After formatting your HDD, you can copy this complete folder, and all it's subfolders to your HDD, then run setup.exe from the CABS folder after booting to to DOS with your boot diskette (you did make a boot diskette, didn't you?).  This will run the install from the HDD, not the CD, and is MUCH faster.  You also have the advantage of not having to insert the CD anytime you make changes to Windows.  It'll just pull the files from the HDD.  Most preloaded PC's come that way from the manufacturer. ||||reg||4
Z||000011||88CoupeV6||06-20-2001||05:07 PM||||What is Windows 95? It must be before my time. I once saw in an old dusty history book something about a...um...Windows 3.1  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>LOL<P>------------------<BR>Josh <B>(known by TheFiero64 for first 1239 posts)</B><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">    <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||12
